{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T13:43:08.881Z","ordinal":41058,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a10c4d-d151-7db0-b6b9-87cdc129232e","call_id":"call_bkSRwMB6Iv89kZ8N2hVr6qaW","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":0,\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"c8171f\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000010319,\"exit_code\":101,\"original_token_count\":330,\"output\":\"• Running devenv:files:cleanup\\n✓ Running devenv:files:cleanup in 13.6ms\\n• Running devenv:files\\n✓ Running devenv:files in 14.4ms\\n• Running devenv:git-hooks:install\\n✓ Running devenv:git-hooks:install in 10.5ms\\n• Running devenv:enterShell\\n✓ Running devenv:enterShell in 10.3ms\\n    Checking twyne-desktop v0.1.0 (/home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/desktop/gpui)\\nerror: this function has too many arguments (8/7)\\n   --> src/app/gen_ui_coordinator.rs:879:5\\n    |\\n879 | /     fn keep_native_tool(\\n880 | |         &mut self,\\n881 | |         scope: ToolScope,\\n882 | |         tool: Uuid,\\n...   |\\n887 | |         cx: &mut Context<Self>,\\n888 | |     ) {\\n    | |_____^\\n    |\\n    = help: for further information visit https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/master/index.html#too_many_arguments\\n    = note: `-D clippy::too-many-arguments` implied by `-D warnings`\\n    = help: to override `-D warnings` add `#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]`\\n\\nerror: could not compile `twyne-desktop` (bin \\\"twyne-desktop\\\") due to 1 previous error\\nwarning: build failed, waiting for other jobs to finish...\\nerror: could not compile `twyne-desktop` (bin \\\"twyne-desktop\\\" test) due to 1 previous error\\n\\n{\\\"job\\\": \\\"/home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/desktop/gpui/target/verification/genui-lint-f9ad938a71d7\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"failed\\\", \\\"exitCode\\\": 101}\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":1,\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"64f7f4\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000018266,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":152,\"output\":\"desktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre/src/text_system.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-base/src/text/style.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre-wgpu/src/cosmic_text_system.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre/src/text_system/line_wrapper.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre/src/text_system/line_layout.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre/src/text_system/line.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre/src/text_system/font_features.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre/src/text_system/font_fallbacks.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-base/src/state_style.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre/src/style.rs\\ndesktop/gpui/vendor/gpui-pre-linux/src/linux/text_system.rs\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":2,\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"48bcfa\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000016039,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":241,\"output\":\"{\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"timed_out\\\",\\n  \\\"provider\\\": \\\"sakana\\\",\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"fugu-max\\\",\\n  \\\"reasoningEffort\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n  \\\"maxCompletionTokens\\\": 20000,\\n  \\\"scope\\\": \\\"advisory-only; no product edits\\\",\\n  \\\"inputSha256\\\": \\\"c1f5e93896279d2a4f932bee80451672891d8a17e82cb51510a083989e3c2690\\\",\\n  \\\"promptSha256\\\": \\\"cf54d62f1dc7ebece3b3915bb6bd2dae77ea0c549602f94330cef5868c61ba9b\\\",\\n  \\\"launcherSha256\\\": \\\"e5f06aa7c3c609b969b73bdb176c89f4a76f6f3889210a89594aa3b328b94a03\\\",\\n  \\\"globalConfigModified\\\": false,\\n  \\\"credential\\\": \\\"privately resolved from Skate; never persisted in config\\\",\\n  \\\"startedUnix\\\": 1791206721.7783616,\\n  \\\"exitCode\\\": null,\\n  \\\"response.txtSha256\\\": \\\"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\\\",\\n  \\\"stderr.txtSha256\\\": \\\"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\\\",\\n  \\\"responseJsonValid\\\": false,\\n  \\\"assistantFinishReasons\\\": [\\n    \\\"tool_use\\\",\\n    \\\"tool_use\\\",\\n    \\\"tool_use\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"elapsedSeconds\\\": 900.116\\n}\\nresponse_bytes 0 stderr_bytes 0\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":3,\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":\"API reference - TypeSafe AI (https://docs.typesafe.ai/api)\\nciteturn81view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://docs.typesafe.ai/api\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 583\\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\\nL1: >\\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\nL5: \\nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \\nL8: cite2†TypeSafe AI home page L9: \\nL10: Search...\\nL11: \\nL12: Navigation\\nL13: \\nL14: Reference\\nL15: \\nL16: API reference\\nL17: \\nL18: Reference\\nL19: # API reference\\nL20: \\nL21: Full HTTP API reference for the TypeSafe evaluation endpoint.\\nL22: \\nL23: Copy page Copy page\\nL24: \\nL25: Evaluate a `state` against a map of typed `questions` and get back structured `answers`, one per question. For a guided introduction, start with the cite3†primitives .\\nL26: \\nL27: ##\\nL28: \\nL29: cite4†​ L30: \\nL31: Evaluation endpoint\\nL32: \\nL33:     `POST https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone\\nL34:     Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>\\nL35:     Content-Type: application/json\\nL36:     `\\nL37: ##\\nL38: \\nL39: cite5†​ L40: \\nL41: Request body\\nL42: \\nL43: The top-level shape of every request. Each entry in the `questions` map is a typed question you name.\\nL44: \\nL45: cite6†​ L46: \\nL47: [Button: state]\\nL48: \\nL49: string | object | array\\nL50: \\nL51: required\\nL52: \\nL53: The content to evaluate. A plain string for text, or structured data (object/array) for things like chat logs, records, or the current state of your application. See cite7†State for formats and best practices.\\nL54: \\nL55: cite8†​ L56: \\nL57: [Button: model]\\nL58: \\nL59: string\\nL60: \\nL61: required\\nL62: The model that handles the request. Use `\\\"jev-latest\\\"`, TypeSafe’s flagship model. See cite9†Models for the available models and aliases.\\nL63: \\nL64: cite10†​ L65: \\nL66: [Button: questions]\\nL67: \\nL68: map<string, Question>\\nL69: \\nL70: required\\nL71: \\nL72: A map of typed cite11†Question objects. You choose each key; answers come back under the same keys.\\nL73: \\nL74: Show map entries\\nL75: \\nL76: cite12†​ L77: \\nL78: [Button: ‹question id›]\\nL79: \\nL80: Question\\nL81: \\nL82: A key you choose. The matching cite13†Answer is returned under this same id. The key is not sent to the underlying model and is not used in inference.\\nL83: Example request\\nL84: \\nL85:     `{\\nL86:       \\\"state\\\": \\\"Help! My payouts have been failing for 3 days.\\\",\\nL87:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-latest\\\",\\nL88:       \\\"questions\\\": {\\nL89:         \\\"is_urgent\\\": {\\nL90:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"noul\\\",\\nL91:           \\\"instructions\\\": \\\"Does this convey urgency?\\\"\\nL92:         }\\nL93:       }\\nL94:     }\\nL95:     `\\nL96: ##\\nL97: \\nL98: cite11†​ L99: \\nL100: Question types\\nL101: A `Question` is one of three types, set by its `type` field. All three share `type` and `instructions`; each adds its own `criteria`. The `instructions` property can be a string, an object, or an array. You can break up a long question that has extra context, or data it needs to reference, into a structured object. Put the question in one field and the data in the others, and refer to the data fields by name in backticks, the same way you point a question at a nested `state` value:\\nL102:     `\\\"instructions\\\": {\\nL103:       \\\"potential_duplicate\\\": {\\nL104:         \\\"name\\\": \\\"John Smith\\\",\\nL105:         \\\"location\\\": \\\"Oakland, California\\\",\\nL106:         \\\"last_employer\\\": \\\"Google\\\"\\nL107:       },\\nL108:       \\\"question\\\": \\\"Is the resume for the same person as `potential_duplicate`?\\\"\\nL109:     }\\nL110:     `\\nL111: See cite14†Use structure in the questions to learn more.\\nL112: ###\\nL113: \\nL114: cite15†​ L115: \\nL116: Noul\\nL117: \\nL118: A yes/no question. Returns the probability the answer is yes.\\nL119: \\nL120: cite16†​ L121: \\nL122: [Button: type]\\nL123: \\nL124: \\\"noul\\\"\\nL125: \\nL126: required\\nL127: \\nL128: cite17†​ L129: \\nL130: [Button: instructions]\\nL131: \\nL132: string | object | array\\nL133: \\nL134: required\\nL135: \\nL136: The yes/no question to evaluate. An object can hold the question in one field and data it refers to in others; see cite14†Use structure in the questions .\\nL137: \\nL138: cite18†​ L139: \\nL140: [Button: criteria]\\nL141: \\nL142: object\\nL143: \\nL144: Optional descriptions of what a yes and a no mean.\\nL145: \\nL146: Show properties\\nL147: \\nL148: cite19†​ L149: \\nL150: [Button: true]\\nL151: \\nL152: string | object | array\\nL153: What a yes (value near 1) means.\\nL154: \\nL155: cite20†​ L156: \\nL157: [Button: false]\\nL158: \\nL159: string | object | array\\nL160: \\nL161: What a no (value near 0) means.\\nL162: Example request\\nL163: \\nL164:     `{\\nL165:       \\\"state\\\": \\\"Help! My payouts have been failing for 3 days.\\\",\\nL166:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-latest\\\",\\nL167:       \\\"questions\\\": {\\nL168:         \\\"is_urgent\\\": {\\nL169:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"noul\\\",\\nL170:           \\\"instructions\\\": \\\"Does this convey urgency?\\\",\\nL171:           \\\"criteria\\\": {\\nL172:             \\\"true\\\": \\\"Explicitly time-sensitive\\\",\\nL173:             \\\"false\\\": \\\"No urgency expressed\\\"\\nL174:           }\\nL175:         }\\nL176:       }\\nL177:     }\\nL178:     `\\nL179: ###\\nL180: \\nL181: cite21†​ L182: \\nL183: Choice\\nL184: \\nL185: Picks one option from a set you define. Returns the chosen option and the full probability distribution.\\nL186: \\nL187: cite22†​ L188: \\nL189: [Button: type]\\nL190: \\nL191: \\\"choice\\\"\\nL192: \\nL193: required\\nL194: \\nL195: cite23†​ L196: \\nL197: [Button: instructions]\\nL198: \\nL199: string | object | array\\nL200: \\nL201: required\\nL202: \\nL203: What the model should decide. An object can hold the question in one field and data it refers to in others; see cite24†Structured instructions and criteria .\\nL204: \\nL205: cite25†​ L206: \\nL207: [Button: criteria]\\nL208: \\nL209: map<string, string | object | array | null>\\nL210: \\nL211: required\\nL212: A map of option to rubric description; use null when an option needs no extra detail. You can have a maximum of 255 options per Choice.\\nL213: \\nL214: Show map entries\\nL215: \\nL216: cite26†​ L217: \\nL218: [Button: ‹option›]\\nL219: \\nL220: string | object | array | null\\nL221: \\nL222: A key you choose. A description of this option.\\nL223: Example request\\nL224: \\nL225:     `{\\nL226:       \\\"state\\\": \\\"Help! My payouts have been failing for 3 days.\\\",\\nL227:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-latest\\\",\\nL228:       \\\"questions\\\": {\\nL229:         \\\"department\\\": {\\nL230:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL231:           \\\"instructions\\\": \\\"Which team should handle this?\\\",\\nL232:           \\\"criteria\\\": {\\nL233:             \\\"billing\\\": \\\"Payments, invoicing, refunds\\\",\\nL234:             \\\"technical\\\": \\\"Bugs, outages, integrations\\\",\\nL235:             \\\"sales\\\": \\\"Pricing, upgrades, new accounts\\\"\\nL236:           }\\nL237:         }\\nL238:       }\\nL239:     }\\nL240:     `\\nL241: ###\\nL242: \\nL243: cite27†​ L244: \\nL245: Score\\nL246: \\nL247: Rates the state along a rubric you define. Returns a probability-weighted value across your levels.\\nL248: \\nL249: cite28†​ L250: \\nL251: [Button: type]\\nL252: \\nL253: \\\"score\\\"\\nL254: \\nL255: required\\nL256: \\nL257: cite29†​ L258: \\nL259: [Button: instructions]\\nL260: \\nL261: string | object | array\\nL262: \\nL263: required\\nL264: \\nL265: What the model should rate. An object can hold the question in one field and data it refers to in others; see cite14†Use structure in the questions .\\nL266: \\nL267: cite30†​ L268: \\nL269: [Button: criteria]\\nL270: \\nL271: array<string | object | array>\\nL272: \\nL273: required\\nL274: An ordered array of level descriptions. A Score should have at least two levels; the API accepts up to 10.\\nL275: \\nL276: Example request\\nL277: \\nL278:     `{\\nL279:       \\\"state\\\": \\\"Help! My payouts have been failing for 3 days.\\\",\\nL280:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-latest\\\",\\nL281:       \\\"questions\\\": {\\nL282:         \\\"frustration\\\": {\\nL283:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"score\\\",\\nL284:           \\\"instructions\\\": \\\"How frustrated is the customer?\\\",\\nL285:           \\\"criteria\\\": [\\\"Calm\\\", \\\"Frustrated\\\", \\\"Very angry\\\"]\\nL286:         }\\nL287:       }\\nL288:     }\\nL289:     `\\nL290: ##\\nL291: \\nL292: cite31†​ L293: \\nL294: Response body\\nL295: \\nL296: One answer per question, returned under the same ids you provided.\\nL297: \\nL298: cite32†​ L299: \\nL300: [Button: model]\\nL301: \\nL302: string\\nL303: \\nL304: required\\nL305: \\nL306: The model that performed the evaluation.\\nL307: \\nL308: cite33†​ L309: \\nL310: [Button: answers]\\nL311: \\nL312: map<string, Answer>\\nL313: \\nL314: required\\nL315: \\nL316: One cite13†Answer per question, keyed by the same ids you used in questions.\\nL317: \\nL318: Show map entries\\nL319: \\nL320: cite34†​ L321: \\nL322: [Button: ‹question id›]\\nL323: \\nL324: Answer\\nL325: \\nL326: The same id you chose in questions.\\nL327: \\nL328: cite35†​ L329: \\nL330: [Button: usage]\\nL331: \\nL332: object\\nL333: \\nL334: required\\nL335: \\nL336: Token usage for the request.\\nL337: \\nL338: Show properties\\nL339: \\nL340: cite36†​ L341: [Button: input_tokens]\\nL342: \\nL343: integer\\nL344: \\nL345: cite37†​ L346: \\nL347: [Button: output_tokens]\\nL348: \\nL349: integer\\nL350: \\nL351: Example response\\nL352: \\nL353:     `{\\nL354:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-1.13.0\\\",\\nL355:       \\\"answers\\\": {\\nL356:         \\\"is_urgent\\\": {\\nL357:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"noul\\\",\\nL358:           \\\"noul\\\": 0.95\\nL359:         }\\nL360:       },\\nL361:       \\\"usage\\\": { \\\"input_tokens\\\": 296, \\\"output_tokens\\\": 20 }\\nL362:     }\\nL363:     `\\nL364: ##\\nL365: \\nL366: cite13†​ L367: \\nL368: Answer types\\nL369: \\nL370: Every answer carries a `type` matching its question. Choice and Score answers also carry a `confidence` between 0 to 1, derived from the answer’s probability distribution. See cite38†Confidence .\\nL371: ###\\nL372: \\nL373: cite39†​ L374: \\nL375: Noul answer\\nL376: \\nL377: cite40†​ L378: \\nL379: [Button: type]\\nL380: \\nL381: \\\"noul\\\"\\nL382: \\nL383: required\\nL384: \\nL385: cite41†​ L386: \\nL387: [Button: noul]\\nL388: \\nL389: number\\nL390: \\nL391: required\\nL392: \\nL393: The yes/no answer on a scale from 0 (no) to 1 (yes).\\nL394: \\nL395: Example response\\nL396: \\nL397:     `{\\nL398:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-1.13.0\\\",\\nL399:       \\\"answers\\\": {\\nL400:         \\\"is_urgent\\\": {\\nL401:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"noul\\\",\\nL402:           \\\"noul\\\": 0.95\\nL403:         }\\nL404:       },\\nL405:       \\\"usage\\\": { \\\"input_tokens\\\": 307, \\\"output_tokens\\\": 20 }\\nL406:     }\\nL407:     `\\nL408: ###\\nL409: \\nL410: cite42†​ L411: \\nL412: Choice answer\\nL413: \\nL414: cite43†​ L415: \\nL416: [Button: type]\\nL417: \\nL418: \\\"choice\\\"\\nL419: \\nL420: required\\nL421: \\nL422: cite44†​ L423: \\nL424: [Button: choice]\\nL425: \\nL426: string\\nL427: \\nL428: required\\nL429: \\nL430: The highest-probability option.\\nL431: \\nL432: cite45†​ L433: \\nL434: [Button: probabilities]\\nL435: \\nL436: map<string, number>\\nL437: \\nL438: required\\nL439: \\nL440: Every option mapped to its probability (floats that sum to 1).\\nL441: \\nL442: Show map entries\\nL443: \\nL444: cite46†​ L445: \\nL446: [Button: ‹option›]\\nL447: \\nL448: number\\nL449: \\nL450: An option you defined in criteria.\\nL451: \\nL452: cite47†​ L453: \\nL454: [Button: confidence]\\nL455: \\nL456: number\\nL457: \\nL458: required\\nL459: \\nL460: How certain the model is, derived from probabilities.\\nL461: Example response\\nL462: \\nL463:     `{\\nL464:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-1.13.0\\\",\\nL465:       \\\"answers\\\": {\\nL466:         \\\"department\\\": {\\nL467:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL468:           \\\"choice\\\": \\\"billing\\\",\\nL469:           \\\"probabilities\\\": { \\\"billing\\\": 0.88, \\\"technical\\\": 0.12, \\\"sales\\\": 0.0 },\\nL470:           \\\"confidence\\\": 0.81\\nL471:         }\\nL472:       },\\nL473:       \\\"usage\\\": { \\\"input_tokens\\\": 318, \\\"output_tokens\\\": 34 }\\nL474:     }\\nL475:     `\\nL476: ###\\nL477: \\nL478: cite48†​ L479: \\nL480: Score answer\\nL481: \\nL482: cite49†​ L483: \\nL484: [Button: type]\\nL485: \\nL486: \\\"score\\\"\\nL487: \\nL488: required\\nL489: \\nL490: cite50†​ L491: \\nL492: [Button: score]\\nL493: \\nL494: number\\nL495: \\nL496: required\\nL497: \\nL498: The probability-weighted answer across the levels; can land between levels.\\nL499: \\nL500: cite51†​ L501: \\nL502: [Button: legend]\\nL503: \\nL504: map<string, string>\\nL505: \\nL506: required\\nL507: \\nL508: Each level number mapped back to its description.\\nL509: \\nL510: cite52†​ L511: \\nL512: [Button: probabilities]\\nL513: \\nL514: map<string, number>\\nL515: \\nL516: required\\nL517: \\nL518: Each level (string key) mapped to its probability (floats that sum to 1).\\nL519: \\nL520: Show map entries\\nL521: \\nL522: cite53†​ L523: \\nL524: [Button: ‹level›]\\nL525: \\nL526: number\\nL527: A level index, as a string key matching legend.\\nL528: \\nL529: cite54†​ L530: \\nL531: [Button: confidence]\\nL532: \\nL533: number\\nL534: \\nL535: required\\nL536: \\nL537: How certain the model is, derived from probabilities.\\nL538: Example response\\nL539: \\nL540:     `{\\nL541:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-1.13.0\\\",\\nL542:       \\\"answers\\\": {\\nL543:         \\\"frustration\\\": {\\nL544:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"score\\\",\\nL545:           \\\"score\\\": 1.05,\\nL546:           \\\"legend\\\": { \\\"0\\\": \\\"Calm\\\", \\\"1\\\": \\\"Frustrated\\\", \\\"2\\\": \\\"Very angry\\\" },\\nL547:           \\\"probabilities\\\": { \\\"0\\\": 0.0, \\\"1\\\": 0.95, \\\"2\\\": 0.05 },\\nL548:           \\\"confidence\\\": 0.92\\nL549:         }\\nL550:       },\\nL551:       \\\"usage\\\": { \\\"input_tokens\\\": 304, \\\"output_tokens\\\": 18 }\\nL552:     }\\nL553:     `\\nL554: ##\\nL555: \\nL556: cite55†​ L557: \\nL558: Errors\\nL559: \\nL560: Errors use standard HTTP status codes with a JSON body describing what went wrong.\\nL561: Status  | Meaning\\nL562: --- | ---\\nL563: `401 Unauthorized`  | Missing or invalid API key. Check the `Authorization` header.\\nL564: `422 Unprocessable Entity`  | The request body failed validation — for example a missing required field or a malformed question. The body details the offending field.\\nL565: `429 Too Many Requests`  | You have exceeded your rate limit. Back off and retry after a short delay.\\nL566: `529 Overloaded`  | TypeSafe is temporarily overloaded. Retry after a short delay.\\nL567: ###\\nL568: \\nL569: cite56†​ L570: \\nL571: Handling rate limits\\nL572: \\nL573: When you receive a `429 Too Many Requests` or `529 Overloaded` response, retry the request with exponential backoff instead of retrying immediately. Our client SDKs handle this automatically, so no extra handling is needed if you use one of our SDKs with its default retry policy.\\nL574: \\nL575: Was this page helpful?\\nL576: \\nL577: Yes No\\nL578: \\nL579: cite9†Models Previous cite57†Agent skill Next L580: \\nL581: cite58†github†github.com cite59†discord†discord.gg cite60†x†x.com L582: cite61†Powered byThis documentation is built and hosted on Mintlify, a developer documentation platform†www.mintlify.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nChoice - TypeSafe AI (https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/choice)\\nciteturn81view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/choice\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 312\\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\\nL1: >\\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\nL5: \\nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \\nL8: cite2†TypeSafe AI home page L9: \\nL10: Search...\\nL11: \\nL12: Navigation\\nL13: \\nL14: Primitives (Questions)\\nL15: \\nL16: Choice\\nL17: \\nL18: Primitives (Questions)\\nL19: # Choice\\nL20: \\nL21: A Choice is a System One question type for selecting one option from a defined set. The answer includes the selected option, a probability for each option, and confidence.\\nL22: \\nL23: Copy page Copy page\\nL24: Use a Choice when the answer is one of a fixed set of options. For example, which team handles a ticket, which category a product belongs to, or which language a code snippet is written in. If the answer is a position on a spectrum, use a cite3†Score . If it’s a yes or no, use a cite4†Noul . cite5†Choose a question type compares all three. A Choice answer is the selected option in `choice`. The model also returns a probability for every option in `probabilities`, and a `confidence` value for the selected option.\\nL25: Example questions:\\nL26: \\nL27:     `\\\"What programming language is this code written in\\\"\\nL28:       → options: python, javascript, typescript, go, rust, other\\nL29: \\nL30:     \\\"What type of meeting is this based on the title and description\\\"\\nL31:       → options: standup, planning, retrospective, one on one, brainstorm, none of the above\\nL32: \\nL33:     \\\"Which product category does this item belong to\\\"\\nL34:       → options: electronics, clothing, home garden, food and beverage\\nL35:     `\\nL36: ##\\nL37: \\nL38: cite6†​ L39: \\nL40: Request structure\\nL41: \\nL42: The POST request body to the cite7†TypeSafe API has a specific structure. The top level has three fields: `state`, the content to evaluate; `model`; and `questions`, a map from question ids you choose to question objects. Each Choice question has the following fields:\\nL43:   * `type`: Always `\\\"choice\\\"`.\\nL44:   * `instructions`: The question the model answers.\\nL45:   * `criteria`: The answer options, as a map. Each key is an option name and each value is a description of that option.\\nL46: Below is a request where the state is a support ticket from an online shoe store and the question is which team should handle it: You choose the question id, `department` in this case. The answer is returned under the same id. The model never sees the question id. The option names and their descriptions are both sent to the model, so write descriptions that separate the options from each other. Our cite8†client SDKs provide typed questions. In Python, the same question is a `Choice`:\\nL47:     `from typesafe_sdk import Choice, TypeSafeClient\\nL48: \\nL49:     with TypeSafeClient() as client:\\nL50:         response = client.system_one(\\nL51:             state=\\\"My running shoes arrived in the wrong size. Can I swap them for a size 10?\\\",\\nL52:             questions={\\nL53:                 \\\"department\\\": Choice(\\nL54:                     instructions=\\\"Which team should handle this?\\\",\\nL55:                     criteria={\\nL56:                         \\\"returns\\\": \\\"Exchanges, wrong or damaged items\\\",\\nL57:                         \\\"shipping\\\": \\\"Delivery status, delays, lost packages\\\",\\nL58:                         \\\"billing\\\": \\\"Charges, invoices, payment problems\\\",\\nL59:                     },\\nL60:                 ),\\nL61:             },\\nL62:         )\\nL63: \\nL64:         print(response.answers[\\\"department\\\"].choice)\\nL65:     `\\nL66: Use the `system_one` method or the `https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone` endpoint to call a System One model. The `model` field selects which model handles the request. cite9†How to build with TypeSafe covers where in your code to call it. Use one of our cite8†client SDKs or call the cite7†HTTP API directly. If a coding agent is writing the integration for you, install the cite10†TypeSafe agent skill first so it knows the request and response shapes.\\nL67: `instructions` and each entry in `criteria` can be a string, an object, or an array. Start with a string. Use an object when a description needs several kinds of guidance, such as what an option covers, what it doesn’t cover, and some examples. See cite11†Structured instructions and criteria below and the cite12†API reference .\\nL68: ##\\nL69: \\nL70: cite13†​ L71: \\nL72: Response structure\\nL73: The response has one entry in `answers` per question, under the ids from the request. This is the response to the example request above:\\nL74: \\nL75:     `{\\nL76:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-1.13.0\\\",\\nL77:       \\\"answers\\\": {\\nL78:         \\\"department\\\": {\\nL79:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL80:           \\\"choice\\\": \\\"returns\\\",\\nL81:           \\\"confidence\\\": 1.0,\\nL82:           \\\"probabilities\\\": {\\nL83:             \\\"shipping\\\": 0.0,\\nL84:             \\\"returns\\\": 1.0,\\nL85:             \\\"billing\\\": 0.0\\nL86:           }\\nL87:         }\\nL88:       },\\nL89:       \\\"usage\\\": {\\nL90:         \\\"input_tokens\\\": 328,\\nL91:         \\\"output_tokens\\\": 34\\nL92:       }\\nL93:     }\\nL94:     `\\nL95: Besides `type`, each Choice answer has three values:\\nL96:   * `choice`: The option with the highest probability.\\nL97:   * `probabilities`: The full probability distribution across every option. The sum of all values is 1.\\nL98:   * cite14†`confidence` : A number from 0 to 1 computed from how `probabilities` is spread. A flat shape, with probability spread across several options, means low confidence. A single peak on one option means high confidence.\\nL99: This ticket is an easy one, so all of the probability is on `returns` and confidence is 1.0. A ticket that mentions a wrong size and a missing refund would split probability between `returns` and `billing`, and confidence would drop.\\nL100: ##\\nL101: \\nL102: cite15†​ L103: \\nL104: Good practice: ask more than one question per call\\nL105: Ask every Choice question your code might need in a single request rather than one request per question. Questions are evaluated in parallel. Adding questions barely changes the response time, and the code can ignore answers it doesn’t need. Extra questions still cost tokens. cite16†Ask multiple questions together explains this in full; the next section shows five Choice questions in one call. The same logic applies to the options inside a single Choice question.\\nL106: A Choice question accepts up to 255 options, and adding options costs a few tokens each, so give the model the full list of teams, categories, or products rather than a shortlist. Add an `other` or `none of the above` option when the list might not cover every input, so the model can say none of the others fit. To classify documents through a deep hierarchy or large taxonomy, chain Choice questions level by level.\\nL107: The cite17†Hierarchical Classification cookbook shows how to run a beam search over Choice probabilities, keeping the best `K` candidate paths at each level instead of committing to a single greedy path.\\nL108: ##\\nL109: \\nL110: cite18†​ L111: \\nL112: A more complex example\\nL113: The basic example above routes a ticket to a team. A bigger support system might also need the return reason, the delivery problem, what the customer wants, and the customer’s tone. The request below asks five Choice questions about a ticket that is more ambiguous than the first: it involves three teams and doesn’t say what the customer wants. Two of these Choice questions are speculative: `return_reason` only matters if the `department` is `returns`, and `shipping_issue` only matters if it’s `shipping`.\\nL114: The `tone` question uses `null` descriptions because the option names are clear on their own. The TypeSafe response:\\nL115: \\nL116:     `{\\nL117:       \\\"model\\\": \\\"jev-1.13.0\\\",\\nL118:       \\\"answers\\\": {\\nL119:         \\\"department\\\": {\\nL120:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL121:           \\\"choice\\\": \\\"returns\\\",\\nL122:           \\\"confidence\\\": 0.42,\\nL123:           \\\"probabilities\\\": {\\nL124:             \\\"shipping\\\": 0.04,\\nL125:             \\\"billing\\\": 0.35,\\nL126:             \\\"returns\\\": 0.61\\nL127:           }\\nL128:         },\\nL129:         \\\"return_reason\\\": {\\nL130:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL131:           \\\"choice\\\": \\\"wrong_size\\\",\\nL132:           \\\"confidence\\\": 1.0,\\nL133:           \\\"probabilities\\\": {\\nL134:             \\\"other\\\": 0.0,\\nL135:             \\\"wrong_size\\\": 1.0,\\nL136:             \\\"changed_mind\\\": 0.0,\\nL137:             \\\"damaged\\\": 0.0,\\nL138:             \\\"wrong_item\\\": 0.0\\nL139:           }\\nL140:         },\\nL141:         \\\"shipping_issue\\\": {\\nL142:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL143:           \\\"choice\\\": \\\"delayed\\\",\\nL144:           \\\"confidence\\\": 0.67,\\nL145:           \\\"probabilities\\\": {\\nL146:             \\\"wrong_address\\\": 0.0,\\nL147:             \\\"other\\\": 0.26,\\nL148:             \\\"not_delivered\\\": 0.0,\\nL149:             \\\"damaged_in_transit\\\": 0.0,\\nL150:             \\\"delayed\\\": 0.74\\nL151:           }\\nL152:         },\\nL153:         \\\"requested_resolution\\\": {\\nL154:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL155:           \\\"choice\\\": \\\"refund\\\",\\nL156:           \\\"confidence\\\": 0.2,\\nL157:           \\\"probabilities\\\": {\\nL158:             \\\"replacement\\\": 0.34,\\nL159:             \\\"refund\\\": 0.4,\\nL160:             \\\"information\\\": 0.02,\\nL161:             \\\"exchange\\\": 0.24\\nL162:           }\\nL163:         },\\nL164:         \\\"tone\\\": {\\nL165:           \\\"type\\\": \\\"choice\\\",\\nL166:           \\\"choice\\\": \\\"frustrated\\\",\\nL167:           \\\"confidence\\\": 0.76,\\nL168:           \\\"probabilities\\\": {\\nL169:             \\\"frustrated\\\": 0.84,\\nL170:             \\\"angry\\\": 0.16,\\nL171:             \\\"calm\\\": 0.0\\nL172:           }\\nL173:         }\\nL174:       },\\nL175:       \\\"usage\\\": {\\nL176:         \\\"input_tokens\\\": 589,\\nL177:         \\\"output_tokens\\\": 212\\nL178:       }\\nL179:     }\\nL180:     `\\nL181: Each question is answered on its own against the ticket:\\nL182:   * The `department` answer is `returns` with a 0.61 probability, but `billing` has 0.35 because of the double charge. The ticket belongs to two teams, and the split confidence of 0.42 reflects that.\\nL183:   * The `return_reason` is `wrong_size` with a confidence of 1.0, which is expected because it says this clearly in the ticket.\\nL184:   * The `shipping_issue` answer is split between `delayed` and `other`. It’s a speculative question and `department` didn’t come back as shipping, so it can be ignored by the code, as shown in the example code snippet below.\\nL185:   * The `requested_resolution` answer leans to `refund` at 0.40, with `replacement` and `exchange` sharing most of the rest, and the confidence is 0.20. The double charge suggests money back, the wrong size suggests a swap, and the customer never says which they want.\\nL186:   * The `tone` answer is `frustrated` with a probability of 0.84 and a confidence of 0.76.\\nL187: The example code below reads the answers it needs, ignores the rest, and treats a low-confidence answer as a reason to ask rather than act:\\nL188: \\nL189:     `from typesafe_sdk import Choice, TypeSafeClient\\nL190: \\nL191:     TRIAGE_QUESTIONS = {\\nL192:         \\\"department\\\": Choice(\\nL193:             instructions=\\\"Which team should handle this?\\\",\\nL194:             criteria={\\nL195:                 \\\"returns\\\": \\\"Exchanges, wrong or damaged items\\\",\\nL196:                 \\\"shipping\\\": \\\"Delivery status, delays, lost packages\\\",\\nL197:                 \\\"billing\\\": \\\"Charges, invoices, payment problems\\\",\\nL198:             },\\nL199:         ),\\nL200:         \\\"return_reason\\\": Choice(\\nL201:             instructions=\\\"If the customer wants to return something, why?\\\",\\nL202:             criteria={\\nL203:                 \\\"wrong_size\\\": \\\"The item doesn't fit\\\",\\nL204:                 \\\"wrong_item\\\": \\\"A different product was delivered\\\",\\nL205:                 \\\"damaged\\\": \\\"The item arrived broken or faulty\\\",\\nL206:                 \\\"changed_mind\\\": \\\"The item is fine, the customer no longer wants it\\\",\\nL207:                 \\\"other\\\": \\\"A return reason that fits none of the above\\\",\\nL208:             },\\nL209:         ),\\nL210:         \\\"shipping_issue\\\": Choice(\\nL211:             instructions=\\\"If this is a shipping problem, which kind is it?\\\",\\nL212:             criteria={\\nL213:                 \\\"not_delivered\\\": \\\"The package never arrived\\\",\\nL214:                 \\\"delayed\\\": \\\"The package is late but still on its way\\\",\\nL215:                 \\\"wrong_address\\\": \\\"The package went to the wrong place\\\",\\nL216:                 \\\"damaged_in_transit\\\": \\\"The package arrived damaged\\\",\\nL217:                 \\\"other\\\": \\\"A shipping problem that fits none of the above\\\",\\nL218:             },\\nL219:         ),\\nL220:         \\\"requested_resolution\\\": Choice(\\nL221:             instructions=\\\"What does the customer want to happen?\\\",\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nConfidence - TypeSafe AI (https://docs.typesafe.ai/confidence)\\nciteturn81view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://docs.typesafe.ai/confidence\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 153\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a10c3a-4441-7890-a25b-a9acd621d3aa","create_time":1791207788.8816304}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
