{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T16:47:54.001Z","ordinal":42785,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a10537-909f-77a3-96d8-dd07161f91ae","turn_id":"01a10cf3-25ca-7a90-895c-0aef2c6d86fb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-292e9023-7d1d-4ab7-b975-3eaca1e855cb","process_id":"97523","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","rtk proxy node /home/diogenes/.agents/skills/impeccable/scripts/context.mjs"],"cwd":"file:///home/diogenes/Projects/twyne","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rtk proxy node /home/diogenes/.agents/skills/impeccable/scripts/context.mjs"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# PRODUCT.md\n\n# Product\n\n## Register\n\nproduct\n\n## Users\n\nWriters, editors, researchers, and technically comfortable creators who want a focused drafting room with structured editorial feedback. They are usually trying to move from a vague brief or partial draft into a clearer manuscript, then export or publish it.\n\n## Product Purpose\n\nTwyne is a local-first writing desk with an AI-assisted room of editors. It helps writers define a project dossier, draft in rich text, receive persona feedback, score work against a rubric, manage citations, sync across devices when signed in, and publish or export finished work. Success means the writer keeps ownership and momentum while the product makes revision concrete.\n\n## Brand Personality\n\nEditorial, exacting, literary. The product should feel like a working writer's room rather than generic SaaS: opinionated, calm, and serious about craft without getting precious.\n\n## Anti-references\n\nDo not make Twyne look like a generic AI chat app, a growth dashboard, a blank note-taking clone, or a loud marketing funnel. Avoid decorative UI that competes with the manuscript, vague AI productivity claims, and controls that obscure where drafts and keys are stored.\n\n## Design Principles\n\n- Keep the manuscript central.\n- Make data movement explicit: local, synced, published, or sent to a provider.\n- Use standard controls for standard tasks, dressed in Twyne's editorial system.\n- Prefer concise copy that names the actual action.\n- Preserve writer trust through clear export, privacy, and account controls.\n\n## Accessibility & Inclusion\n\nAim for WCAG AA contrast and keyboard-operable controls. Important interactions should work with semantic HTML where possible. Respect reduced-motion preferences, avoid color-only state, and keep long-form reading comfortable on mobile and desktop.\n\n---\n\nNEXT STEP: This project's register is `product`. You MUST now read `reference/product.md` before producing any design output.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# PRODUCT.md\n\n# Product\n\n## Register\n\nproduct\n\n## Users\n\nWriters, editors, researchers, and technically comfortable creators who want a focused drafting room with structured editorial feedback. They are usually trying to move from a vague brief or partial draft into a clearer manuscript, then export or publish it.\n\n## Product Purpose\n\nTwyne is a local-first writing desk with an AI-assisted room of editors. It helps writers define a project dossier, draft in rich text, receive persona feedback, score work against a rubric, manage citations, sync across devices when signed in, and publish or export finished work. Success means the writer keeps ownership and momentum while the product makes revision concrete.\n\n## Brand Personality\n\nEditorial, exacting, literary. The product should feel like a working writer's room rather than generic SaaS: opinionated, calm, and serious about craft without getting precious.\n\n## Anti-references\n\nDo not make Twyne look like a generic AI chat app, a growth dashboard, a blank note-taking clone, or a loud marketing funnel. Avoid decorative UI that competes with the manuscript, vague AI productivity claims, and controls that obscure where drafts and keys are stored.\n\n## Design Principles\n\n- Keep the manuscript central.\n- Make data movement explicit: local, synced, published, or sent to a provider.\n- Use standard controls for standard tasks, dressed in Twyne's editorial system.\n- Prefer concise copy that names the actual action.\n- Preserve writer trust through clear export, privacy, and account controls.\n\n## Accessibility & Inclusion\n\nAim for WCAG AA contrast and keyboard-operable controls. Important interactions should work with semantic HTML where possible. Respect reduced-motion preferences, avoid color-only state, and keep long-form reading comfortable on mobile and desktop.\n\n---\n\nNEXT STEP: This project's register is `product`. You MUST now read `reference/product.md` before producing any design output.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":39206618},"formatted_output":"# PRODUCT.md\n\n# Product\n\n## Register\n\nproduct\n\n## Users\n\nWriters, editors, researchers, and technically comfortable creators who want a focused drafting room with structured editorial feedback. They are usually trying to move from a vague brief or partial draft into a clearer manuscript, then export or publish it.\n\n## Product Purpose\n\nTwyne is a local-first writing desk with an AI-assisted room of editors. It helps writers define a project dossier, draft in rich text, receive persona feedback, score work against a rubric, manage citations, sync across devices when signed in, and publish or export finished work. Success means the writer keeps ownership and momentum while the product makes revision concrete.\n\n## Brand Personality\n\nEditorial, exacting, literary. The product should feel like a working writer's room rather than generic SaaS: opinionated, calm, and serious about craft without getting precious.\n\n## Anti-references\n\nDo not make Twyne look like a generic AI chat app, a growth dashboard, a blank note-taking clone, or a loud marketing funnel. Avoid decorative UI that competes with the manuscript, vague AI productivity claims, and controls that obscure where drafts and keys are stored.\n\n## Design Principles\n\n- Keep the manuscript central.\n- Make data movement explicit: local, synced, published, or sent to a provider.\n- Use standard controls for standard tasks, dressed in Twyne's editorial system.\n- Prefer concise copy that names the actual action.\n- Preserve writer trust through clear export, privacy, and account controls.\n\n## Accessibility & Inclusion\n\nAim for WCAG AA contrast and keyboard-operable controls. Important interactions should work with semantic HTML where possible. Respect reduced-motion preferences, avoid color-only state, and keep long-form reading comfortable on mobile and desktop.\n\n---\n\nNEXT STEP: This project's register is `product`. You MUST now read `reference/product.md` before producing any design output.\n"},"started_at_ms":1791218873959,"completed_at_ms":1791218873999}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T16:47:54.115Z","ordinal":42786,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a10537-909f-77a3-96d8-dd07161f91ae","turn_id":"01a10cf3-25ca-7a90-895c-0aef2c6d86fb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-b384d72c-4b6a-4d6a-be7f-3554d90066ca","process_id":"76955","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","rtk proxy bun desktop/gpui/tools/build-connected.ts desktop/gpui/target/verification/connected-sdk-browser-build-20261005-c"],"cwd":"file:///home/diogenes/Projects/twyne","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rtk proxy bun desktop/gpui/tools/build-connected.ts desktop/gpui/target/verification/connected-sdk-browser-build-20261005-c"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"  [30ms]  minify  -0.98 MB (estimate)\n   [9ms]  bundle  220 modules\n [168ms] compile  /home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/desktop/gpui/target/verification/connected-sdk-browser-build-20261005-c/twyne-connected\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"  [30ms]  minify  -0.98 MB (estimate)\n   [9ms]  bundle  220 modules\n [168ms] compile  /home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/desktop/gpui/target/verification/connected-sdk-browser-build-20261005-c/twyne-connected\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":142967910},"formatted_output":"  [30ms]  minify  -0.98 MB (estimate)\n   [9ms]  bundle  220 modules\n [168ms] compile  /home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/desktop/gpui/target/verification/connected-sdk-browser-build-20261005-c/twyne-connected\n"},"started_at_ms":1791218873972,"completed_at_ms":1791218874115}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T16:47:54.225Z","ordinal":42787,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a10cf6-f771-73b0-ad97-ecc8ad0e84eb","call_id":"call_ub7WwErhq36yzzVbw5ObaBHa","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.4 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":0,\"result\":{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"894172\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.038855265,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":492,\"output\":\"# PRODUCT.md\\n\\n# Product\\n\\n## Register\\n\\nproduct\\n\\n## Users\\n\\nWriters, editors, researchers, and technically comfortable creators who want a focused drafting room with structured editorial feedback. They are usually trying to move from a vague brief or partial draft into a clearer manuscript, then export or publish it.\\n\\n## Product Purpose\\n\\nTwyne is a local-first writing desk with an AI-assisted room of editors. It helps writers define a project dossier, draft in rich text, receive persona feedback, score work against a rubric, manage citations, sync across devices when signed in, and publish or export finished work. Success means the writer keeps ownership and momentum while the product makes revision concrete.\\n\\n## Brand Personality\\n\\nEditorial, exacting, literary. The product should feel like a working writer's room rather than generic SaaS: opinionated, calm, and serious about craft without getting precious.\\n\\n## Anti-references\\n\\nDo not make Twyne look like a generic AI chat app, a growth dashboard, a blank note-taking clone, or a loud marketing funnel. Avoid decorative UI that competes with the manuscript, vague AI productivity claims, and controls that obscure where drafts and keys are stored.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\n- Keep the manuscript central.\\n- Make data movement explicit: local, synced, published, or sent to a provider.\\n- Use standard controls for standard tasks, dressed in Twyne's editorial system.\\n- Prefer concise copy that names the actual action.\\n- Preserve writer trust through clear export, privacy, and account controls.\\n\\n## Accessibility & Inclusion\\n\\nAim for WCAG AA contrast and keyboard-operable controls. Important interactions should work with semantic HTML where possible. Respect reduced-motion preferences, avoid color-only state, and keep long-form reading comfortable on mobile and desktop.\\n\\n---\\n\\nNEXT STEP: This project's register is `product`. You MUST now read `reference/product.md` before producing any design output.\\n\"}}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":1,\"result\":{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"1e3ba7\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000037857,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":940,\"output\":\"# Product register\\n\\nWhen design SERVES the product: app UIs, admin dashboards, settings panels, data tables, tools, authenticated surfaces, anything where the user is in a task.\\n\\n## The product slop test\\n\\nNot \\\"would someone say AI made this.\\\" Familiarity is often a feature here. The test is: would a user fluent in the category's best tools (Linear, Figma, Notion, Raycast, Stripe come to mind) sit down and trust this interface, or pause at every subtly-off component?\\n\\nProduct UI's failure mode isn't flatness, it's strangeness without purpose: over-decorated buttons, mismatched form controls, gratuitous motion, display fonts where labels should be, invented affordances for standard tasks. The bar is earned familiarity. The tool should disappear into the task.\\n\\n## Typography\\n\\n- **One family is often right.** Product UIs don't need display/body pairing. A well-tuned sans carries headings, buttons, labels, body, data.\\n- **Fixed rem scale, not fluid.** Clamp-sized headings don't serve product UI. Users view at consistent DPI, and a fluid h1 that shrinks in a sidebar looks worse, not better.\\n- **Tighter scale ratio.** 1.125–1.2 between steps is typical. More type elements here than on brand surfaces; exaggerated contrast creates noise.\\n- **Line length still applies for prose** (65–75ch). Data and compact UI can run denser; tables at 120ch+ are fine.\\n\\n## Color\\n\\nProduct defaults to Restrained. A single surface can earn Committed (a dashboard where one category color carries a report, an onboarding flow with a drenched welcome screen), but Restrained is the floor.\\n\\n- State-rich semantic vocabulary: hover, focus, active, disabled, selected, loading, error, warning, success, info. Standardize these.\\n- Accent color used for primary actions, current selection, and state indicators only, not decoration.\\n- A second neutral layer for sidebars, toolbars, and panels (slightly cooler or warmer than the content surface).\\n\\n## Layout\\n\\n- Responsive behavior is structural (collapse sidebar, responsive table, breakpoint-driven columns), not fluid typography.\\n\\n## Components\\n\\nEvery interactive component has: default, hover, focus, active, disabled, loading, error. Don't ship with half of these.\\n\\n- Skeleton states for loading, not spinners in the middle of content.\\n- Empty states that teach the interface, not \\\"nothing here.\\\"\\n- Consistent affordances across the surface. Same button shape. Same form-control vocabulary. Same icon style.\\n\\n## Motion\\n\\n- 150–250 ms on most transitions. Users are in flow; don't make them wait for choreography.\\n- Motion conveys state, not decoration. State change, feedback, loading, reveal: nothing else.\\n- No orchestrated page-load sequences. Product loads into a task; users don't want to watch it load.\\n\\n## Product bans (on top of the shared absolute bans)\\n\\n- Decorative motion that doesn't convey state.\\n- Inconsistent component vocabulary across screens. If the \\\"save\\\" button looks different in two places, one is wrong.\\n- Display fonts in UI labels, buttons, data.\\n- Reinventing standard affordances for flavor (custom scrollbars, weird form controls, non-standard modals).\\n- Heavy color or full-saturation accents on inactive states.\\n- Modal as first thought. Modals are usually laziness. Exhaust inline / progressive alternatives first.\\n\\n## Product permissions\\n\\nProduct can afford things brand surfaces can't.\\n\\n- System fonts and familiar sans defaults (Inter, SF Pro, system-ui stacks).\\n- Standard navigation patterns: top bar + side nav, breadcrumbs, tabs, command palettes.\\n- Density. Tables with many rows, panels with many labels, dense information when users need it.\\n- Consistency over surprise. The same visual vocabulary screen to screen is a virtue; delight is saved for moments, not pages.\\n\"}}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":2,\"result\":{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"4d6f9f\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000035116,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":0,\"output\":\"\"}}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":3,\"result\":{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"c118f5\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.142990707,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":51,\"output\":\"  [30ms]  minify  -0.98 MB (estimate)\\n   [9ms]  bundle  220 modules\\n [168ms] compile  /home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/desktop/gpui/target/verification/connected-sdk-browser-build-20261005-c/twyne-connected\\n\"}}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":4,\"result\":{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"7c5e7c\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.004558677,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":24,\"output\":\"/home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/desktop/gpui/target/verification/connected-delegation-20261005-c\\n\"}}}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a10cf3-25ca-7a90-895c-0aef2c6d86fb","create_time":1791218874.225862}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
