kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791150511.0590055,"turn_id":"01a108e3-ab20-77b3-b852-58c490ac55cb"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"opus is out of usage","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a108e5-1797-7be0-a4bd-63130dcdced9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791150593.943457,"turn_id":"01a108e4-f455-7461-9208-abb47793512b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what are some other areas of the port you have not worked on","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10918-0df2-7d63-8ed5-3255097c7c8f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791153933.8105314,"turn_id":"01a108e4-f455-7461-9208-abb47793512b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"spin up subagents for all the things you havent work on in parallel you can also do things like give smaller tasks to crush or open code delegate delegate delegate","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1091a-e7e6-7893-b20e-1f7ee5d63c6d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791154120.6786976,"turn_id":"01a108e4-f455-7461-9208-abb47793512b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"gemini 3.8 flash can also do work too","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1091b-7d76-7723-95cd-6392a5bb0303","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791154158.9663599,"turn_id":"01a108e4-f455-7461-9208-abb47793512b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"these agents are significantly less capable than you are so keep this in mind","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10924-dfdb-70a3-82b5-344ea360b21d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791154773.9792206,"turn_id":"01a108e4-f455-7461-9208-abb47793512b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"use the sakana models","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10925-85a2-7101-bacf-e18be69ef1e5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791154816.418173,"turn_id":"01a108e4-f455-7461-9208-abb47793512b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"max","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10925-c998-73d0-9750-3b88f5b6659a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791154833.816269,"turn_id":"01a108e4-f455-7461-9208-abb47793512b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"sakana key in skate","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10931-8cb5-7ee3-8b64-1eee53ca7038","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791155604.6618588,"turn_id":"01a10931-6b4d-7c72-953d-7b1209c0cbbd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":".","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1094e-fdd9-74b1-ba79-fdf8b8804d27","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791157534.1695395,"turn_id":"01a1094e-b771-7df1-ad6f-c626cab96d12"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":".","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10a1c-0a9a-76d0-862e-5312c97a9f56","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791170972.3148634,"turn_id":"01a10a1b-b650-7660-b5ef-56e1a3784ac1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"status report","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10b52-46ed-7f41-959e-f106ea1871fb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791191303.917562,"turn_id":"01a10a1b-b650-7660-b5ef-56e1a3784ac1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"okay and what about online writing and sync?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10b53-da43-7270-80cd-7083547d38fa","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791191407.1716025,"turn_id":"01a10a1b-b650-7660-b5ef-56e1a3784ac1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what about the ai integration? knowledge base integration, gen ui components?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10b54-68e0-7e10-a51e-719395495580","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791191443.6800916,"turn_id":"01a10a1b-b650-7660-b5ef-56e1a3784ac1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"start the application i want to see what its like","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10b57-fa01-7481-83c6-cd438ad53be0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791191677.4412775,"turn_id":"01a10a1b-b650-7660-b5ef-56e1a3784ac1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"some interface bugs i noticed the title doesn't fully appear, getting cutoff on the bottom and top text, seems like some other field instead of just part of the same document.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10d17-d63e-7422-b308-733ebf06665a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791221028.4143982,"turn_id":"01a10d17-989f-7150-9fa5-0c502f3fed47"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"slash commands also don't seem to work yet, when typing / a menu should appear like on web with different options for me to input corresponding to different elements in a typist/markdown document like headers, tables, links, etc. just like the editor on web","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a10d2c-8564-7413-b861-aca58880da0a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791222383.9727714,"turn_id":"01a10d17-989f-7150-9fa5-0c502f3fed47"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -\u003e specific):\n\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/skills/\u003cskill-name\u003e/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally \u003c= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `\u003cfile\u003e:\u003cline_start\u003e-\u003cline_end\u003e|note=[\u003chow memory was used\u003e]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `\u003crollout_ids\u003e` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `\u003ctimestamp\u003e-\u003cshort slug\u003e.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /home/diogenes/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nThe user builds and ships interconnected TypeScript/Bun products including Keating, Twyne, Not Organic, Baste, Stich, dio-machines, Interleave, and Traction, using React/Qwik, Convex, Railway, Nix/Devenv, Rust, and agent tooling. They value source-backed autonomous collaboration that preserves dirty work and distinguishes deterministic checks from browser, provider, payment, deployment, release, clean-install, and device proof. They provide an intended experience and expect concrete implementation judgment, inspectable visual directions, accessible UI, and truthful completion boundaries. Not Organic is the account/control plane for shared identity, authorization, account evolution, and connected clients; sandbox/microVM runners are replaceable adapters. [ad-hoc note]\n\n## User preferences\n\n- Start from the “intent behind the solution ... instead of describing the fix”; translate experience/context into implementation criteria.\n- Preserve unrelated dirty work; inspect and explicitly establish scope before commits, releases, deployment, upgrades, cleanup, or broadly attributing impact.\n- Treat proof boundaries literally: tests/builds/HTTP 200/deployment do not establish browser, provider, payment, device, or production proof.\n- For ambiguous visual work, give concrete ideas, pictures/probes, component examples, and a plan; ask focused questions, present 2–4 directions, and wait for confirmation before coding.\n- When typing responsiveness is “unacceptable,” profile the real interaction and validate the feel in browser/production; preserve editor/proof/PDF parity for editorial changes.\n- For connected knowledge, make sources account-owned with guided consent, per-app grants, explicit revocation, and server-held credentials.\n- For video briefs, “don’t just try once—do several different videos”: make genuinely distinct audience-specific cuts; when re-serving, test MP4 seeking as well as the gallery page.\n- For a readiness question such as “how far are we from continual learning as a service,” lead with a concrete estimate, separate shipped primitives from unverified service gates, and name the next milestone.\n- For visual/video work, when asked to render it “to check them out,” deliver a watchable preview rather than only describing source.\n- No external email/send action without explicit authorization; retain drafts and ask before sending.\n\n## General Tips\n\n- Use current `rg`/source inspection alongside graph tools; GitNexus may be stale, lock-failed, or truncated. Confirm missing graph edges with text search.\n- A dev error page can return HTTP 200: inspect expected DOM markers and console; restart the specific Vite/SSR process if an import now exists.\n- Keep PKCE/DPoP, sessions, upstream credentials, and gateway transport server-side; enforce scopes/capabilities, CSRF, origins, and catalog inputs fail-closed.\n- Keep editor keystroke paths free of whole-document work; use explicit freshness boundaries and separate cold readiness from latency samples.\n- Validate Typst exports with the real compiler plus PDF text/rendered-page inspection; metadata alone can mislead.\n- For local video review, require HTTP 206/`Accept-Ranges` checks; an HTML/video 200 is insufficient to prove seeking.\n- For GPUI work, pin/recheck the exact core and GPUI Kit version; GPUI remains pre-1.0.\n\n## What's in Memory\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/baste\n\n#### 2026-10-04\n\n- Makepad versus GPUI studio framework: Makepad, GPUI, GPUI Kit, Rust GUI, Zed, candidate grids\n  - desc: Direct framework-choice guidance for Baste’s native studio in cwd=/home/diogenes/Projects/baste.\n  - learnings: Default to GPUI for dense keyboard-driven projects/inspectors/queues; choose Makepad for expressive GPU graphics and shared web/mobile surfaces.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/notorganic-provider\n\n#### 2026-10-04\n\n- Continual-learning service readiness: continual-learning, AccountEvolutionService, Tinker, save_weights_for_sampler, active-revision, checkpoint serving\n  - desc: Evidence-based gap assessment between Not Organic account evolution and managed continual learning, with Keating/Tinker pilot evidence.\n  - learnings: Infrastructure is substantial but hosted training, checkpoint-aware inference, repeated evaluation/promotion/rollback, billing, and customer controls remain unproven; a two-cycle authorized Keating pilot is the first credible milestone.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/interleave-app\n\n#### 2026-10-04\n\n- Remotion sizzle assembly and preview render: InterleaveSizzle, Sizzle.tsx, Remotion, artifacts/sizzle-preview-v3, CapturedProductDemo.tsx\n  - desc: Assembly of existing sizzle scenes into registered landscape/portrait compositions and an inspectable MP4 preview.\n  - learnings: Use focused sizzle checks and stills/short clips before costly full rendering; existing `CapturedProductDemo.tsx` errors were unrelated.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/notorganic-provider\n\n#### 2026-10-03\n\n- Shared account knowledge connections: knowledge-bases, account-sources, PKCE S256, DPoP, Google Drive, account-tool-review\n  - desc: Provider-owned account knowledge and partial Keating/Twyne integrations; search first for consent, revocation, source selection, or MCP review.\n  - learnings: Google Drive is “Coming soon”; focused checks passed but deployment, real-account, and cross-app gateway proof remain open.\n\n- Explainer films and range-aware review gallery: HyperFrames, Cartesia, Seedance, Lyria, serve@14.2.6, HTTP 206\n  - desc: Three verified Not Organic masters, brand capture, completion audit, and a local range-seekable multi-project review server.\n  - learnings: Use the standalone `serve@14.2.6` gallery server rather than `http.server`/single-project Studio when sibling MP4 routes must seek.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/twyne\n\n#### 2026-10-03\n\n- Editor responsiveness and Typst export parity: Tiptap, ProseMirror, flushVisual, typing-latency, drop-cap, twyne-wrap-opening\n  - desc: Measured typing-stall removal and proof/PDF drop-cap wrapping in cwd=/home/diogenes/Projects/twyne.\n  - learnings: Defer reconciliation outside typing, flush at explicit freshness boundaries, activate Proof intentionally, and verify PDFs with compiler/text/rendered output.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/interleave-app\n\n#### 2026-10-03\n\n- Warm tactile design direction: less corporrate and cold, generic tailwindy, handmade tech, thread-weave, gecko adhesion\n  - desc: Discovery-first redesign guidance for warm, sensible Interleave components.\n  - learnings: Exploration is incomplete—confirm a compact brief after 2–4 probes before implementation; shared tokens are in `packages/ui/src/styles.css`.\n\n### /home/diogenes/Projects/baste\n\n#### 2026-10-02\n\n- Side-conversation recovery, saved Projects, and Not Organic scaffolding: /side, parent thread, Cannot find module, devenv processes restart site, repair-lock\n  - desc: Baste plan recovery, account-scoped Projects persistence, stale-SSR repair, and server-side provider boundaries.\n  - learnings: A Vite error page may return 200; inspect `studio-main` and console before calling the preview healthy.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### /home/diogenes/Projects/keating\n\n- Product architecture, learning policy, account controls, releases, docs, media, and visual identity: TabPFN, CatBoost, prompt=select_account, AccessAdmin, Railway, Keatingbot\n  - desc: Search for teaching/runtime truth, provider boundaries, launch/release proof, documentation/Textbook, or Keating visual direction; Keatingbot is the canonical image anchor. [ad-hoc note]\n\n#### /home/diogenes/Projects/twyne\n\n- Editorial product, Typst, Jev, provider, launch-film, and release workflows: Typst, Jev, Not Organic, HyperFrames, Railway, Bun versioning\n  - desc: Detailed editor, writing-tool, identity, deployment, and release task families for cwd=/home/diogenes/Projects/twyne.\n\n#### /home/diogenes/Projects/notorganic-provider\n\n- Provider architecture, billing, legal, launches, rules, and private release: PKCE, DPoP, Convex, SandboxGateway, Paddle, Radicle, account evolution\n  - desc: Fail-closed provider control-plane and commercialization history; account evolution remains provider-owned while runners are adapter-neutral. [ad-hoc note]\n\n#### /home/diogenes/Projects/stich\n\n- Inbox product, multi-account identity, Devenv, pricing, release, and Railway mail workflows: Electrobun, JMAP, Creem, Railway, Radicle\n  - desc: Stich application and operational task families; recheck active provider/mail state before claims.\n\n#### /home/diogenes/Projects/dio-machines and cross-repository tooling\n\n- Devenv, Nix, MCP, desktop, terminal identity, and shared hooks: devenv.nix, Fnox, Skate, git-hooks.nix, terminal-logo-rasterizer\n  - desc: Machine/tooling workflows and reusable terminal-brand procedure; use `skills/terminal-logo-rasterizer/SKILL.md` for raster-to-Braille work. [ad-hoc note]\n\n#### Other products and research\n\n- Textbook, Voxq, Traction, dio.computer, CAD, HyperFrames, and research artifacts: textbook, Voxq, Traction, Typst, video\n  - desc: Search `MEMORY.md` by project cwd for task-family detail and current applicability boundaries.\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/home/diogenes/.agents/skills`\n- `r2` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r3` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r4` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.16/skills`\n- `r5` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/pages/0.1.19/skills`\n- `r6` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r7` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/0.1.75/skills`\n- `r8` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/work-pets/0.1.6/skills`\n- `r9` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r10` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.905.11957/skills`\n- `r11` = `/home/diogenes/.codex/plugins/cache/typesafe-ai`\n- `r12` = `/home/diogenes/Projects/twyne/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, te (file: r2/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and (file: r2/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r2/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list ins (file: r2/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- arize-admin: Manages Arize users, organizations, spaces, projects, roles, role bindings, resource restrictions, and API keys via the (file: r12/arize-admin/SKILL.md)\n- arize-ai-provider-integration: Creates, reads, updates, and deletes Arize AI integrations that store LLM provider credentials used by evaluators and o (file: r12/arize-ai-provider-integration/SKILL.md)\n- arize-annotation: Creates and manages annotation configs (categorical, continuous, freeform label schemas) and annotation queues (human (file: r12/arize-annotation/SKILL.md)\n- arize-compliance-audit: INVOKE THIS SKILL when auditing an AI agent or LLM app for regulatory compliance. Covers EU AI Act, GPAI Code of Prac (file: r12/arize-compliance-audit/SKILL.md)\n- arize-dataset: Creates, manages, and queries Arize datasets and examples. Covers dataset CRUD, appending examples, exporting data, and (file: r12/arize-dataset/SKILL.md)\n- arize-evaluator: Handles LLM-as-judge and code evaluator workflows on Arize including creating/updating evaluators, running evaluations  (file: r12/arize-evaluator/SKILL.md)\n- arize-experiment: Creates, runs, and analyzes Arize experiments for evaluating and comparing model performance. Covers experiment CRUD, (file: r12/arize-experiment/SKILL.md)\n- arize-instrumentation: Adds Arize AX tracing to an LLM application for the first time. Follows a two-phase agent-assisted flow to analyze the  (file: r12/arize-instrumentation/SKILL.md)\n- arize-link: Generates deep links to the Arize UI for traces, spans, sessions, datasets, labeling queues, evaluators, and annotati (file: r12/arize-link/SKILL.md)\n- arize-prompt-optimization: Optimizes, improves, and debugs LLM prompts using production trace data, evaluations, and annotations. Extracts prompts (file: r12/arize-prompt-optimization/SKILL.md)\n- arize-prompts: INVOKE THIS SKILL for Arize Prompt Hub and `ax prompts` workflows: author or import templates and save (Workflows A–B (file: r12/arize-prompts/SKILL.md)\n- arize-trace: Downloads, exports, and inspects existing Arize traces and spans to understand what an LLM app is doing or debug runtim (file: r12/arize-trace/SKILL.md)\n- convex: Convex is the backend agents get right on the first try: an all-TypeScript reactive platform where the database, serv (file: r12/convex/SKILL.md)\n- convex-acquire-domain: Find and buy a domain for the current Convex app through Convex, then bind it (labs; spend action). (file: r12/convex-acquire-domain/SKILL.md)\n- convex-add: Add a capability to the CURRENT Convex app — consults the served Convex capability catalog for always-current procedure (file: r12/convex-add/SKILL.md)\n- convex-advisor: Read the Convex deployment's 72h insights (read limits, OCC contention), root-cause each event in code, report eviden (file: r12/convex-advisor/SKILL.md)\n- convex-agent: Add an AI agent / RAG backend (@convex-dev/agent) to the Convex app. (file: r12/convex-agent/SKILL.md)\n- convex-auth: Add authentication (passkeys/OAuth) to the current Convex app, including the auth.config.ts wiring. (file: r12/convex-auth/SKILL.md)\n- convex-authz: Audit and harden Convex authorization: identity-from-arg impersonation, missing per-document ownership checks, PII-le (file: r12/convex-authz/SKILL.md)\n- convex-backup: Set up Convex backups and run a restore DRILL that proves recovery — snapshot, restore into a throwaway preview, asse (file: r12/convex-backup/SKILL.md)\n- convex-billing: Add Stripe billing/payments to the Convex app via @convex-dev/stripe (checkout + webhook + gating). (file: r12/convex-billing/SKILL.md)\n- convex-check-updates: Check the current app's pinned Convex components against recommended versions and upgrade them behind a build gate. (file: r12/convex-check-updates/SKILL.md)\n- convex-cost: Preview Convex spend — rank functions by bytes/documents-read × call-volume from insights, project each cost driver's gr (file: r12/convex-cost/SKILL.md)\n- 