"Insert\", /* std::option::Option<assets::TwyneIcon> */, cx).on_click(cx.listener(|this, _, _, cx| this.submit(cx)))))\n    |                                                                       +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n\nSome errors have detailed explanations: E0061, E0599.\nFor more information about an error, try `rustc --explain E0061`.\nwarning: `twyne-desktop` (lib) generated 1 warning\nerror: could not compile `twyne-desktop` (lib) due to 3 previous errors; 1 warning emitted\n"},"started_at_ms":1791225730091,"completed_at_ms":1791225765541}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T18:42:57.044Z","ordinal":44158,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a10537-909f-77a3-96d8-dd07161f91ae","turn_id":"01a10d4c-a644-7d71-9054-bf5cd3583d03","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_0acb6e766bb0227d016ac3efa5638887d1bb662e5d0ec4c071","summary_text":[],"raw_content":[]},"started_at_ms":1791225765432,"completed_at_ms":1791225777044}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T18:42:57.050Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"id":"rs_0acb6e766bb0227d016ac3efa5638887d1bb662e5d0ec4c071","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a10d4c-a644-7d71-9054-bf5cd3583d03"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T18:43:09.621Z","ordinal":44160,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a10537-909f-77a3-96d8-dd07161f91ae","turn_id":"01a10d4c-a644-7d71-9054-bf5cd3583d03","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0acb6e766bb0227d016ac3efb0ff0c87d192d699fd59784831","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"I’m structuring it around features and document boundaries, with `app.rs` responsible for assembling the writing room. It still carries too much rendering and lifecycle code; the existing coordinators help, and I’m moving new work out of it consistently.\n\n| Area | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `src/app/` | Connect features to the active manuscript, focus, routing, and saving |\n| `src/editor/` | Rich document model, transactions, history, selection, and native editor views |\n| `src/insert_menu/` | Slash catalog, detection, filtering, Markdown/rich insertion adapters, menu, and forms |\n| `workspace`, `source_session`, `semantic_bridge` | Authored Typst and its visual projection |\n| `storage`, `revisions` | Local persistence and document history |\n| `native_accounts`, `tools/connected` | Rust account contracts and the Bun SDK boundary |\n\nMy grasp is strongest around the editor, document preservation, local saving, and how the GPUI screen connects those pieces. I also know where the connected-work scaffolding stops: online sync and live AI/knowledge-base integration still need host wiring and real-account verification. I’m not treating prepared APIs or passing unit tests as completed features.\n\nThe biggest improvements for working faster here are:\n\n- **A searchable module map:** Rust module → corresponding web component → relevant tests. I’m adding that so investigations start at the right boundary.\n- **Reproducible UI fixtures:** a known document, window size, scale, and editor mode, exercised in an isolated library. That makes clipping and keyboard bugs much easier to reproduce.\n- **Smaller delegation units:** pure filtering, placement, conversion, or contract tasks with frozen inputs. Sakana’s review found useful Markdown edge cases, but also made a stale-query claim that conflicts with the web editor’s caret behavior—so delegated output needs direct checks.\n- **Separate implementation from evidence:** record which build was actually inspected, rather than assuming an older screenshot proves current code.\n\nThe next structural step is to separate the writing-room layout from its lifecycle orchestration. I’m finishing the slash interaction first so that extraction follows working boundaries."}],"phase":"commentary"},"started_at_ms":1791225777053,"completed_at_ms":1791225789621}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-05T18:43:09.624Z","ordinal":44161,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_0acb6e766bb0227d016ac3efb0ff0c87d192d699fd59784831","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I’m structuring it around features and document boundaries, with `app.rs` responsible for assembling the writing room. It still carries too much rendering and lifecycle code; the existing coordinators help, and I’m moving new work out of it consistently.\n\n| Area | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `src/app/` | Connect features to the active manuscript, focus, routing, and saving |\n| `src/editor/` | Rich document model, transactions, history, selection, and native editor views |\n| `src/insert_menu/` | Slash catalog, detection, filtering, Markdown/rich insertion adapters, menu, and forms |\n| `workspace`, `source_session`, `semantic_bridge` | Authored Typst and its visual projection |\n| `storage`, `revisions` | Local persistence and document history |\n| `native_accounts`, `tools/connected` | Rust account contracts and the Bun SDK boundary |\n\nMy grasp is strongest around the editor, document preservation, local saving, and how the GPUI screen connects those pieces. I also know where the connected-work scaffolding stops: online sync and live AI/knowledge-base integration still need host wiring and real-account verification. I’m not treating prepared APIs or passing unit tests as completed features.\n\nThe biggest improvements for working faster here are:\n\n- **A searchable module map:** Rust module → corresponding web component → relevant tests. I’m adding that so investigations start at the right boundary.\n- **Reproducible UI fixtures:** a known document, window size, scale, and editor mode, exercised in an isolated library. That makes clipping and keyboard bugs much easier to reproduce.\n- **Smaller delegation units:** pure filtering, placement, conversion, or contract tasks with frozen inputs. Sakana’s review found useful Markdown edge cases, but also made a stale-query claim that conflicts with the web editor’s caret behavior—so delegated output needs direct checks.\n- **Separate implementation from evidence:** record which build was actually inspected, rather than assuming an older screenshot proves current code.\n\nThe next structural step is to separate the writing-room layout from its lifecycle orchestration. I’m finishing the slash interaction first so that extraction follows working boundaries."}],"phase":"commentary","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a10d4c-a644-7d71-9054-bf5cd3583d03","create_time":1791225740.109687,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}},"metadata":{"retained_source":{"id":{"message_id":"msg_0acb6e766bb0227d016ac3efb0ff0c87d192d699fd59784831","turn_id":"01a10d4c-a644-7d71-9054-bf5cd3583d03","role":"assistant"},"revision":"retained_01a10d60-7cb8-7313-83d0-73ba29b5d590","complete":true},"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":2382}}
